1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:05,462
Before a place becomes familiar, sometimes there is just a color.

2
00:00:05,412 --> 00:00:07,618
A band of blue.

3
00:00:07,618 --> 00:00:09,897
A darker green.

4
00:00:09,897 --> 00:00:13,176
A little white along a distant ridge.

5
00:00:13,348 --> 00:00:19,166
For the next while, if you like, let those colors gather into a landscape.

6
00:00:19,116 --> 00:00:25,072
You can follow the pictures on the screen, or allow your own version to arrive.

7
00:00:25,072 --> 00:00:27,733
A suggestion is enough.

8
00:00:27,892 --> 00:00:30,768
Find a comfortable position.

9
00:00:30,718 --> 00:00:34,027
Your breathing can keep its own pace.

10
00:00:34,027 --> 00:00:36,866
Your eyes can remain open.

11
00:00:42,036 --> 00:00:46,707
Picture a peninsula with mountains rising through its center.

12
00:00:46,657 --> 00:00:50,083
Forests gather along the valleys.

13
00:00:50,083 --> 00:00:54,995
Rivers find their way outward, toward the surrounding water.

14
00:00:54,995 --> 00:01:03,054
Along the Pacific shore, another narrow part of Olympic National Park follows the meeting of land and sea.

15
00:01:03,204 --> 00:01:11,580
We will travel through these places in imagination, moving between settings that a real journey would take time to reach.

16
00:01:11,530 --> 00:01:18,604
Around the park are towns, Tribal lands, working forests and other public lands.

17
00:01:18,604 --> 00:01:22,295
The view is larger than any one boundary.

18
00:01:22,452 --> 00:01:26,490
Let your attention settle on a river for a moment.

19
00:01:26,440 --> 00:01:30,102
There is movement in that thin line of water.

20
00:01:30,102 --> 00:01:35,396
There are also human relationships you cannot see from this distance.

21
00:01:39,556 --> 00:01:45,285
Before following the river, make room for the people whose homelands these are.

22
00:01:45,388 --> 00:01:49,162
Keep the outline of the peninsula in mind.

23
00:01:49,112 --> 00:02:03,744
Now hear the names of eight contemporary Tribes with longstanding connections here: Lower Elwha Klallam, Jamestown S’Klallam, Port Gamble S’Klallam, Skokomish, Quinault, Hoh, Quileute and Makah.

24
00:02:07,916 --> 00:02:12,542
Each name belongs to a distinct community and government.

25
00:02:12,492 --> 00:02:18,904
Their histories and relationships with these places began long before the national park.

26
00:02:19,076 --> 00:02:22,129
Return your attention to the river.

27
00:02:22,079 --> 00:02:27,873
From far away, you might first notice its color, or the shape of a bend.

28
00:02:27,873 --> 00:02:38,314
For people whose lives are connected to it, a river can also be a fishery, a route, a place of family connection and a focus of work today.

29
00:02:38,468 --> 00:02:42,786
Allow the picture to hold more than the visible surface.

30
00:02:42,736 --> 00:02:46,956
A scenic view offers one way of knowing a place.

31
00:02:46,956 --> 00:02:54,294
The Tribes’ own accounts carry relationships and histories that give these waters another depth of meaning.

32
00:02:57,452 --> 00:03:03,078
Those living relationships bring the idea of protection into the present.

33
00:03:03,028 --> 00:03:12,440
Caring for a place involves people making decisions, often across the boundaries that a river or a bird moves through so easily.

34
00:03:12,596 --> 00:03:16,840
Picture a bird crossing a stretch of coastal water.

35
00:03:16,790 --> 00:03:19,849
Its wings settle into a rhythm.

36
00:03:19,849 --> 00:03:25,599
Ahead is an offshore rock; farther out, the water where it may feed.

37
00:03:25,748 --> 00:03:29,786
Follow that small movement across the wider scene.

38
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:37,718
On a map, responsibilities meet here.

39
00:03:37,668 --> 00:03:46,094
Along Olympic’s coast, intertidal areas also lie within the Olympic Coast National Marine Sanctuary.

40
00:03:46,094 --> 00:03:53,079
Offshore islands are part of the Washington Maritime National Wildlife Refuge Complex.

41
00:03:53,079 --> 00:04:01,124
A nesting place and a feeding place can belong to the same animal’s life while falling within different areas of care.

42
00:04:01,284 --> 00:04:10,087
The park’s memorandum of understanding with eight peninsula Tribes provides a framework for government-to-government coordination.

43
00:04:10,037 --> 00:04:15,463
Each government retains its own responsibilities and perspective.

44
00:04:15,636 --> 00:04:19,718
Let the bird reach the rock in your imagined scene.

45
00:04:19,668 --> 00:04:23,271
Its route makes one connection visible.

46
00:04:23,271 --> 00:04:28,271
Human agreements help people work across connections like these.

47
00:04:31,428 --> 00:04:37,187
Care also extends to objects, records and cultural places.

48
00:04:37,137 --> 00:04:49,960
Farther along this coast, Ozette brings that kind of responsibility into focus: how a place’s material history is understood, and who carries its interpretation forward.

49
00:04:50,124 --> 00:04:56,030
Allow the wide coast to give way to the quieter attention of a museum visit.

50
00:04:55,980 --> 00:05:03,480
Picture yourself pausing before an exhibit, giving one object more time than you first expected.

51
00:05:03,636 --> 00:05:10,395
You might notice its outline, the texture of its material, or evidence of careful work.

52
00:05:10,345 --> 00:05:17,580
As your eye follows those details, the museum’s interpretation begins to give them context.

53
00:05:21,748 --> 00:05:31,080
The Makah Cultural and Research Center interprets materials from the Ozette village archaeological site through a Makah institution.

54
00:05:31,030 --> 00:05:38,619
The objects belong within an account of Makah life, with living descendants and continuing knowledge.

55
00:05:38,788 --> 00:05:42,223
Looking closely can begin a question.

56
00:05:42,173 --> 00:05:48,129
An object’s appearance alone cannot answer everything about its use or meaning.

57
00:05:48,129 --> 00:05:56,644
Museum interpretation, community knowledge and archaeological evidence each bring something to the understanding.

58
00:05:56,812 --> 00:06:04,615
Stay with that patient way of looking: notice what is present, then listen for the context that gives it meaning.

59
00:06:07,732 --> 00:06:15,373
Our attention now turns toward a different kind of record, far older than the objects of a human life.

60
00:06:15,323 --> 00:06:19,132
Picture a line of mountains beyond the coast.

61
00:06:19,132 --> 00:06:24,147
Some of the rock raised into those ridges began beneath the sea.

62
00:06:24,316 --> 00:06:29,928
For a moment, let the ocean become transparent in your imagination.

63
00:06:29,878 --> 00:06:35,790
Beneath it, picture the edge of a continent and a moving plate of Earth’s crust.

64
00:06:35,956 --> 00:06:41,818
This is a model, unfolding far faster than the processes it represents.

65
00:06:41,932 --> 00:06:49,191
Offshore, the Juan de Fuca plate moves beneath North America at the Cascadia subduction zone.

66
00:06:49,141 --> 00:06:56,111
Along that converging edge, material can be scraped together, compressed and folded.

67
00:06:56,111 --> 00:07:01,347
Geologists call the gathered mass an accretionary complex.

68
00:07:05,516 --> 00:07:08,481
Let the view widen through time.

69
00:07:08,431 --> 00:07:11,416
Rock is lifted and deformed.

70
00:07:11,416 --> 00:07:15,431
Water and ice wear into the rising landscape.

71
00:07:15,431 --> 00:07:23,960
Olympic’s basalt and sedimentary rocks include material with offshore origins, now part of a mountain world.

72
00:07:24,116 --> 00:07:30,051
Basalt can preserve a history of volcanic activity on an ancient seafloor.

73
00:07:30,001 --> 00:07:35,737
That does not make the Olympic range a chain of volcanoes like the Cascades.

74
00:07:35,737 --> 00:07:41,840
The formation of the rock and the raising of the mountains are different parts of this story.

75
00:07:44,996 --> 00:07:48,858
Bring the mountains back beneath an open sky.

76
00:07:48,808 --> 00:07:52,543
Their valleys hold another record of movement.

77
00:07:52,543 --> 00:07:56,955
Follow one downward, toward the blue of Lake Crescent.

78
00:07:57,116 --> 00:07:59,919
Let a lakeshore take shape.

79
00:08:00,020 --> 00:08:03,117
There is blue between the mountains.

80
00:08:03,067 --> 00:08:06,964
Near the shore, it becomes transparent.

81
00:08:06,964 --> 00:08:13,846
Pale stones rest beneath the surface, their edges wavering as a ripple passes over them.

82
00:08:14,012 --> 00:08:18,594
Imagine standing where that small wave reaches the land.

83
00:08:18,544 --> 00:08:23,838
You might give it a sound: a soft touch of water against stone.

84
00:08:23,838 --> 00:08:27,250
Then a little space before the next one.

85
00:08:32,404 --> 00:08:34,869
Look across the lake.

86
00:08:34,819 --> 00:08:41,525
Follow the mountain upward, from the dark trees along the shore to the ridge against the sky.

87
00:08:41,525 --> 00:08:46,686
Now let your attention travel down its reflection, into the blue.

88
00:08:46,852 --> 00:08:50,214
This basin holds the work of ice.

89
00:08:50,164 --> 00:08:57,002
Long before this imagined morning, glaciers eroded and carried rock through the landscape.

90
00:08:57,002 --> 00:08:59,267
The ice withdrew.

91
00:08:59,267 --> 00:09:01,620
The hollow remained.

92
00:09:01,620 --> 00:09:14,046
Elsewhere in Olympic, glacial valleys and mountain cirques preserve related traces, although mountain glaciers and the continental ice sheet followed different paths and histories.

93
00:09:18,212 --> 00:09:21,574
The lake’s connections changed again.

94
00:09:21,524 --> 00:09:31,038
The National Park Service describes a massive landslide about seven thousand years ago that separated Lake Crescent from Lake Sutherland.

95
00:09:31,038 --> 00:09:37,538
Isolation helped produce the distinctive Beardslee and Crescenti trout populations.

96
00:09:37,700 --> 00:09:41,768
Picture a fish moving through that reflected mountain.

97
00:09:41,718 --> 00:09:47,247
The shape and history of the basin have become part of the story of its life.

98
00:09:47,420 --> 00:09:51,017
Then return to the small wave at your feet.

99
00:09:50,967 --> 00:09:59,717
The lake offers both scales at once: a ripple that lasts a moment, and a landscape carrying a much longer history.

100
00:10:05,876 --> 00:10:10,400
Above the water, picture a cloud brushing the ridge.

101
00:10:10,350 --> 00:10:19,511
As your attention follows it, the mountains begin another story: what happens when air from the Pacific meets this rising land.

102
00:10:19,676 --> 00:10:23,023
Follow that cloud toward the mountains.

103
00:10:22,973 --> 00:10:29,532
Give the air a direction, arriving from the ocean and rising with the slopes beneath it.

104
00:10:29,684 --> 00:10:35,178
As moist air rises, it cools and releases precipitation.

105
00:10:35,128 --> 00:10:39,246
Western slopes intercept much of that moisture.

106
00:10:39,246 --> 00:10:45,246
Farther northeast, a rain shadow brings much drier regional conditions.

107
00:10:49,404 --> 00:10:59,501
Picture the difference as two views held beside each other: moisture gathering against one side of the mountains, and a drier landscape beyond.

108
00:10:59,451 --> 00:11:06,054
The broad pattern helps explain how such different environments fit within one peninsula.

109
00:11:06,228 --> 00:11:10,604
An individual afternoon still has its own weather.

110
00:11:10,554 --> 00:11:18,746
Elevation, the direction a slope faces and the path of a storm add differences within that larger pattern.

111
00:11:18,900 --> 00:11:24,776
Now allow a little of the arriving water to remain high on the mountain as snow.

112
00:11:24,726 --> 00:11:27,521
Some will melt with the season.

113
00:11:27,521 --> 00:11:31,315
Some becomes part of ice held for longer.

114
00:11:34,476 --> 00:11:39,470
Water can move quickly through a landscape, or wait within it.

115
00:11:39,420 --> 00:11:47,317
On a glacier, the balance between what arrives and what leaves begins to shape the future of that stored ice.

116
00:11:47,484 --> 00:11:51,860
Picture snow settling onto a broad surface of ice.

117
00:11:51,810 --> 00:11:59,222
Let the falling flakes fill the near distance, then allow the scene to move forward into the melt season.

118
00:11:59,388 --> 00:12:05,838
A glacier gains mass through snowfall and loses it through melting and other processes.

119
00:12:05,788 --> 00:12:11,479
When losses repeatedly exceed gains, its store of ice diminishes.

120
00:12:11,479 --> 00:12:15,759
The end may retreat while the ice itself grows thinner.

121
00:12:19,924 --> 00:12:26,727
The two Blue Glacier photographs in this journey show eighteen ninety-nine and two thousand eight.

122
00:12:26,677 --> 00:12:30,280
They offer a historical comparison.

123
00:12:30,280 --> 00:12:36,912
Repeated measurements help reveal changes that a pair of outlines cannot fully describe.

124
00:12:37,084 --> 00:12:40,313
Give your attention a path downhill.

125
00:12:40,263 --> 00:12:46,793
Imagine meltwater leaving the ice and joining a stream during the relatively dry summer.

126
00:12:46,793 --> 00:12:52,852
Other water reaches that stream too; the mixture differs among watersheds.

127
00:12:53,020 --> 00:12:56,338
There is good evidence of ice loss.

128
00:12:56,288 --> 00:13:02,611
Its local effects on future summer flow need more than one answer for the whole park.

129
00:13:05,764 --> 00:13:09,479
Keep that question gently in the background.

130
00:13:09,429 --> 00:13:15,090
For now, let the white slopes open into green patches and meadow light.

131
00:13:15,090 --> 00:13:18,958
Our imagined view moves to Hurricane Ridge.

132
00:13:19,108 --> 00:13:21,926
Give the scene room to open.

133
00:13:21,876 --> 00:13:28,067
Picture a meadow beside a stand of trees, with ridges continuing beyond it.

134
00:13:28,067 --> 00:13:34,582
You might imagine wind passing through the low plants while the darker forest holds its shape.

135
00:13:34,732 --> 00:13:40,608
Let your attention move from the distant mountains to a few flowers near the ground.

136
00:13:44,708 --> 00:13:48,629
Here, small differences in position matter.

137
00:13:48,579 --> 00:13:51,564
Height influences temperature.

138
00:13:51,564 --> 00:13:58,432
The direction a slope faces changes the sunlight it receives and how long snow can remain.

139
00:13:58,432 --> 00:14:05,505
Those conditions help determine where plants establish and how much time they have to grow each year.

140
00:14:05,660 --> 00:14:10,272
The meeting of trees and open ground forms a patchwork.

141
00:14:10,222 --> 00:14:14,031
Follow its irregular edge with your attention.

142
00:14:14,031 --> 00:14:20,134
A sheltered pocket can offer different conditions from an exposed slope nearby.

143
00:14:20,300 --> 00:14:24,250
Black-tailed deer may graze in this high country.

144
00:14:24,200 --> 00:14:30,185
Wildflowers and growing plants make use of a season that snow helps define.

145
00:14:33,356 --> 00:14:36,865
Hold that brief mountain summer in mind.

146
00:14:36,815 --> 00:14:41,124
A small opening among the plants suggests a burrow.

147
00:14:41,124 --> 00:14:46,741
Another life here must gather much of what it needs before winter returns.

148
00:14:46,892 --> 00:14:51,357
Picture an Olympic marmot near the entrance to its burrow.

149
00:14:51,307 --> 00:14:54,174
Give it a little space in the scene.

150
00:14:54,174 --> 00:15:01,910
You can notice the shape of its body against the meadow, the shelter close behind it, the plants within reach.

151
00:15:06,060 --> 00:15:11,848
This species occurs naturally in the Olympic Mountains and nowhere else.

152
00:15:11,798 --> 00:15:18,254
That is what endemic means: its natural range belongs to this particular region.

153
00:15:18,420 --> 00:15:23,164
A long hibernation carries the marmot through much of the year.

154
00:15:23,114 --> 00:15:29,217
The active season provides time to feed, reproduce and build reserves.

155
00:15:29,217 --> 00:15:37,555
Olympic marmots mature relatively slowly for rodents, so lost breeding adults can take time to replace.

156
00:15:37,716 --> 00:15:41,416
Now widen the view beyond this one burrow.

157
00:15:41,366 --> 00:15:45,307
Imagine other meadow patches among the mountains.

158
00:15:45,307 --> 00:15:51,734
Repeated surveys help reveal where marmots remain, disappear or return.

159
00:15:51,734 --> 00:15:58,616
The park has identified concerns involving predation and changing habitat conditions.

160
00:16:01,788 --> 00:16:09,209
One encounter can hold your attention; understanding the species asks for many observations.

161
00:16:09,159 --> 00:16:12,217
We will return to that difference.

162
00:16:12,217 --> 00:16:19,894
For now, allow the meadow light to soften as the journey moves into the layered green of the Hoh rainforest.

163
00:16:20,052 --> 00:16:23,723
Let the forest form around a little light.

164
00:16:23,673 --> 00:16:31,643
Picture it catching on the edge of a leaf, then passing through other leaves until the ground below is softly shaded.

165
00:16:31,812 --> 00:16:36,233
You might imagine the air here as cool against your face.

166
00:16:36,183 --> 00:16:39,403
Look upward in your imagined scene.

167
00:16:39,403 --> 00:16:43,756
Branches carry more green than their own leaves alone.

168
00:16:47,908 --> 00:16:55,961
Bigleaf maples support mosses and ferns along their limbs, while conifers rise above the forest floor.

169
00:16:55,911 --> 00:17:00,029
An epiphyte is a plant growing on another plant.

170
00:17:00,029 --> 00:17:05,043
Its position gives it that name; it need not be a parasite.

171
00:17:05,043 --> 00:17:11,087
Trunks and branches become additional places for life within the larger forest.

172
00:17:11,260 --> 00:17:14,254
Follow the light downward again.

173
00:17:14,204 --> 00:17:20,160
Where a tree has fallen, an opening can change what reaches the plants below.

174
00:17:20,160 --> 00:17:26,042
The canopy, the trunk and the ground offer different small habitats.

175
00:17:26,212 --> 00:17:32,177
The Hoh River links this valley with the Mount Olympus high country and the Pacific.

176
00:17:32,127 --> 00:17:40,215
Hoh belongs alongside Queets, Bogachiel and Quinault among Olympic’s western rainforest valleys.

177
00:17:40,215 --> 00:17:43,362
Each retains its own character.

178
00:17:46,540 --> 00:17:50,416
Carry that attention to layers into the Queets.

179
00:17:50,366 --> 00:17:57,499
There, a fallen tree can bring the forest’s generations within the span of a single close look.

180
00:17:57,652 --> 00:18:01,028
Imagine a fallen trunk beside you.

181
00:18:00,978 --> 00:18:05,126
Its length draws a quiet line through the forest.

182
00:18:05,126 --> 00:18:13,670
Along the softened wood, picture a small seedling, its green standing clear against the darker surface beneath it.

183
00:18:18,828 --> 00:18:26,866
In the Queets valley, large Sitka spruce and moss-draped maples grow around the remains of earlier trees.

184
00:18:26,816 --> 00:18:32,978
Elk browse in this wider setting and help shape the vegetation beneath the canopy.

185
00:18:33,132 --> 00:18:35,685
Return to the seedling.

186
00:18:35,635 --> 00:18:40,561
A decaying trunk that supports new growth is called a nurse log.

187
00:18:40,561 --> 00:18:45,885
As a young tree develops, its roots can reach down into the soil.

188
00:18:45,885 --> 00:18:53,664
When the older wood eventually disappears, a line of trees and raised roots may preserve its former place.

189
00:18:53,820 --> 00:19:02,402
Let time move gently through that image: the old trunk softening, roots extending, a new crown reaching toward light.

190
00:19:02,352 --> 00:19:07,073
Fungi and invertebrates also use the decomposing wood.

191
00:19:07,073 --> 00:19:14,088
Growth, feeding and decay happen together, though every fallen tree follows its own course.

192
00:19:18,244 --> 00:19:22,562
A forest can carry its past in the shapes you see.

193
00:19:22,512 --> 00:19:25,468
Wood leaves one kind of record.

194
00:19:25,468 --> 00:19:30,923
Fire leaves another, especially toward the peninsula’s drier side.

195
00:19:31,084 --> 00:19:39,078
Allow the scene to move eastward in imagination, into forest shaped by different moisture and exposure.

196
00:19:39,028 --> 00:19:43,881
Keep the trees in view while considering what they may have lived through.

197
00:19:44,044 --> 00:19:49,685
Picture a tree ring, a scar in wood, a dark fragment of charcoal.

198
00:19:49,635 --> 00:19:53,459
Each can hold evidence of an earlier event.

199
00:19:57,620 --> 00:20:05,335
Olympic’s eastern forests have generally experienced fire more frequently than the wetter western forests.

200
00:20:05,285 --> 00:20:08,299
Even a wet forest can burn.

201
00:20:08,299 --> 00:20:15,358
What happens in a particular event depends on weather, fuels and the surrounding landscape.

202
00:20:15,524 --> 00:20:23,518
Let the standing forest become one moment within a longer sequence: disturbance, growth and recovery.

203
00:20:23,468 --> 00:20:28,453
A familiar green view may contain several histories at once.

204
00:20:28,604 --> 00:20:34,245
A past interval between fires is not a timetable for the next fire.

205
00:20:34,195 --> 00:20:42,107
The evidence helps us understand how a place developed while leaving its future responsive to changing conditions.

206
00:20:45,260 --> 00:20:52,431
Keep those differences in mind as the journey reaches Staircase, in the park’s southeastern corner.

207
00:20:52,381 --> 00:21:00,616
There, the North Fork Skokomish passes through a forest with a different character from the wetter valleys we have left.

208
00:21:00,788 --> 00:21:04,650
Picture Douglas-firs rising around a river.

209
00:21:04,600 --> 00:21:11,835
Let your attention find the spaces between the trunks, then the moving brightness of water beyond them.

210
00:21:11,996 --> 00:21:17,858
At Staircase, the North Fork Skokomish offers another side of Olympic.

211
00:21:17,808 --> 00:21:25,940
Moisture, exposure and elevation help shape a forest setting distinct from the moss-heavy western valleys.

212
00:21:30,108 --> 00:21:34,764
Choose a small patch of moving water in your imagined view.

213
00:21:34,714 --> 00:21:39,596
Follow it past a stone, around a bend, into the shade.

214
00:21:39,596 --> 00:21:43,258
Now give the river more room and more time.

215
00:21:43,258 --> 00:21:46,361
Water levels rise and fall.

216
00:21:46,361 --> 00:21:49,082
Floods move material.

217
00:21:49,082 --> 00:21:53,361
Banks and vegetation interact with that movement.

218
00:21:53,532 --> 00:22:02,908
Rain, snowmelt and water delayed or stored within the watershed contribute in changing proportions among seasons and rivers.

219
00:22:02,858 --> 00:22:07,020
The channel before you is part of that wider system.

220
00:22:07,188 --> 00:22:18,285
You can look closely at one reach while allowing the river to continue beyond the picture, toward higher valleys and lakes in one direction, downstream in the other.

221
00:22:21,396 --> 00:22:27,493
Across the peninsula, Sol Duc offers another view of water meeting rock.

222
00:22:27,443 --> 00:22:33,458
Let the low sound of this imagined river give way to the broader rush of a waterfall.

223
00:22:33,612 --> 00:22:39,268
Picture water reaching a ledge, gathering at its edge, then falling.

224
00:22:39,218 --> 00:22:44,924
You might imagine its sound arriving before the whole waterfall comes into view.

225
00:22:45,084 --> 00:22:52,431
Let your attention follow the movement downward, then rest on a quieter part of the water beyond it.

226
00:22:57,548 --> 00:23:05,704
The Sol Duc landscape connects old-growth forest, waterfalls, subalpine lakes and snowy peaks.

227
00:23:05,654 --> 00:23:13,404
Along a river corridor, pools, faster channels and sheltered margins offer different conditions.

228
00:23:13,404 --> 00:23:18,889
Large wood can redirect flow and help form pools and side channels.

229
00:23:18,889 --> 00:23:24,419
Shade and connections within the watershed also influence habitat.

230
00:23:24,572 --> 00:23:28,728
These relationships vary from reach to reach.

231
00:23:28,678 --> 00:23:36,031
One waterfall is an entrance into the river’s story, rather than a picture of everything it provides.

232
00:23:36,188 --> 00:23:44,270
At Sol Duc’s Salmon Cascades, migrating coho can be seen negotiating a natural step in the river.

233
00:23:44,220 --> 00:23:51,382
Imagine the contrast between a sheltered pocket of water and the movement needed to pass a cascade.

234
00:23:55,548 --> 00:23:59,983
The meaning of each place changes with the life using it.

235
00:23:59,933 --> 00:24:10,477
To follow a salmon’s journey, allow the scene to become smaller and quieter: water passing through freshwater gravel, around developing eggs.

236
00:24:10,644 --> 00:24:14,300
Begin with the spaces between stones.

237
00:24:14,250 --> 00:24:19,368
Picture water moving through gravel, where salmon eggs develop.

238
00:24:19,368 --> 00:24:25,662
Keep the scene simple enough to hold: stones, water, the beginning of a life.

239
00:24:29,812 --> 00:24:32,747
Now let time move forward.

240
00:24:32,697 --> 00:24:38,668
Young salmon use freshwater rearing habitats before moving toward the sea.

241
00:24:38,668 --> 00:24:46,756
The word anadromous describes fish that spend part of their lives at sea and return to freshwater to reproduce.

242
00:24:46,924 --> 00:24:51,712
Allow the route to widen through an estuary into the ocean.

243
00:24:51,662 --> 00:24:55,442
Each setting can matter at a different stage.

244
00:24:55,442 --> 00:25:00,089
A place used briefly may still be essential to the journey.

245
00:25:00,244 --> 00:25:04,885
Species and populations differ in timing and needs.

246
00:25:04,835 --> 00:25:12,526
Sol Duc coho and Quinault sockeye belong to this larger account without following identical schedules.

247
00:25:12,526 --> 00:25:16,718
The park contains only part of these moving lives.

248
00:25:20,876 --> 00:25:25,238
Let an adult fish turn inland in your imagination.

249
00:25:25,188 --> 00:25:31,673
A return on one day cannot describe every condition shaping a population’s future.

250
00:25:31,673 --> 00:25:43,408
But the returning fish carries a visible connection between worlds: material acquired in the ocean is now moving upstream, into the life of the river and forest.

251
00:25:43,580 --> 00:25:47,133
Keep that returning fish within the scene.

252
00:25:47,083 --> 00:25:52,083
Around it, allow the river’s banks to become forest again.

253
00:25:52,083 --> 00:25:56,156
There are lives here that will use what the fish brings.

254
00:25:56,324 --> 00:26:00,568
Salmon carry marine-derived nutrients inland.

255
00:26:00,518 --> 00:26:04,312
Predators and scavengers feed on fish.

256
00:26:04,312 --> 00:26:09,048
Decomposition opens other paths into the food web.

257
00:26:09,048 --> 00:26:18,827
In the Queets valley, eagles, otters and smaller organisms participate in these connections among river, wildlife and forest.

258
00:26:22,980 --> 00:26:27,033
Picture the movement as a set of branching paths.

259
00:26:26,983 --> 00:26:29,954
Some follow a feeding animal.

260
00:26:29,954 --> 00:26:33,042
Some become too small to see.

261
00:26:33,042 --> 00:26:39,983
This is an imagined model of the connection; the quantities and routes vary from place to place.

262
00:26:40,140 --> 00:26:45,208
Recall the nurse log, with new roots reaching through older wood.

263
00:26:45,158 --> 00:26:50,511
Here too, living and once-living material is being rearranged.

264
00:26:50,511 --> 00:26:58,261
Water carries nutrients downstream, while fish can carry material acquired at sea back inland.

265
00:27:01,428 --> 00:27:07,510
Within that broad pattern, particular fish still need particular places.

266
00:27:07,460 --> 00:27:11,152
Let the forest open toward Lake Quinault.

267
00:27:11,152 --> 00:27:18,666
Its blueback salmon have a story tied to this lake, this watershed and the Quinault Indian Nation.

268
00:27:18,828 --> 00:27:22,513
Allow a lake to open between the trees.

269
00:27:22,463 --> 00:27:29,096
You might give its surface a soft movement, just enough to gather and loosen a reflection.

270
00:27:29,268 --> 00:27:40,394
Here at Lake Quinault, blueback are a sockeye salmon population with deep cultural, ecological and economic importance to the Quinault Indian Nation.

271
00:27:40,344 --> 00:27:46,065
Their life cycle connects this lake and river system with the ocean beyond it.

272
00:27:50,236 --> 00:27:54,348
Picture the lake as a place within that moving life.

273
00:27:54,298 --> 00:28:01,651
Freshwater residence gives it a role that a simple arrow from river to ocean cannot fully describe.

274
00:28:01,651 --> 00:28:07,033
The Nation’s fisheries work follows the needs of these particular fish.

275
00:28:07,204 --> 00:28:14,507
Earlier, Lake Crescent showed how a basin’s history and isolation could influence its trout.

276
00:28:14,457 --> 00:28:20,398
Let that memory sit beside this different lake, without merging their stories.

277
00:28:20,398 --> 00:28:27,295
The details of a place matter to the lives it supports and the people responsible for its care.

278
00:28:30,460 --> 00:28:37,969
Lake Quinault will return near the end of our journey as an open question guided by the Nation’s assessments.

279
00:28:37,919 --> 00:28:45,860
First, attention moves to the Elwha, where removing two dams changed the connections available to a river.

280
00:28:46,012 --> 00:28:50,344
Picture a river corridor with a barrier across it.

281
00:28:50,294 --> 00:28:57,103
Hold that shape for a moment, then let the view change to the river after the barrier’s removal.

282
00:28:57,268 --> 00:29:02,850
The Elwha’s two dams were removed in work that began in two thousand eleven.

283
00:29:02,800 --> 00:29:08,300
Removal of Glines Canyon Dam was completed in two thousand fourteen.

284
00:29:08,300 --> 00:29:13,050
Passage reopened after roughly a century of obstruction.

285
00:29:13,050 --> 00:29:20,962
For the Lower Elwha Klallam Tribe, the restoration belongs within a much longer relationship with the river.

286
00:29:25,124 --> 00:29:29,736
Allow the before-and-after view to keep moving through time.

287
00:29:29,686 --> 00:29:32,289
Sediment is released.

288
00:29:32,289 --> 00:29:36,245
Former reservoir surfaces are exposed.

289
00:29:36,245 --> 00:29:42,127
Revegetation and floodplain restoration become part of continuing work.

290
00:29:42,127 --> 00:29:47,877
Fish, plants, floods and sediment respond on different timescales.

291
00:29:48,044 --> 00:29:52,670
The removal of a structure changes what can happen next.

292
00:29:52,620 --> 00:29:59,812
Understanding that response requires returning to the river after the dramatic moment has passed.

293
00:30:02,972 --> 00:30:07,496
Follow the imagined river beyond a former dam site.

294
00:30:07,446 --> 00:30:10,034
There is now a way through.

295
00:30:10,034 --> 00:30:17,357
How can researchers tell which fish have used it, how far they have gone, and what their return means?

296
00:30:17,516 --> 00:30:25,966
Picture a stretch of water observed more than once: before the dams were removed, then during the years that followed.

297
00:30:25,916 --> 00:30:29,607
Each visit adds another part to the record.

298
00:30:29,780 --> 00:30:36,230
Studies have compared fish distributions along the Elwha before and after removal.

299
00:30:36,180 --> 00:30:43,224
Another method looks for environmental DNA: traces that organisms shed into the water.

300
00:30:43,388 --> 00:30:50,912
For a moment, imagine a small water sample holding clues too fine for your eyes to resolve.

301
00:30:55,020 --> 00:31:04,367
Species-specific tests found evidence that most of the targeted migratory fish species had passed both former dam sites.

302
00:31:04,317 --> 00:31:08,979
The timing and upstream extent differed among species.

303
00:31:09,132 --> 00:31:13,597
A detection can support an account of use or passage.

304
00:31:13,547 --> 00:31:23,194
By itself, it does not tell us the number of fish, whether they reproduced successfully, or how resilient the population will be.

305
00:31:23,194 --> 00:31:27,429
Different questions need different kinds of evidence.

306
00:31:30,588 --> 00:31:38,112
There is room to recognize the significance of the reopened route while continuing to ask about recovery.

307
00:31:38,062 --> 00:31:41,620
Now follow the water downstream again.

308
00:31:41,620 --> 00:31:48,414
Along with signs of living things, the river carries grains of the landscape toward the sea.

309
00:31:48,588 --> 00:31:53,082
Choose a grain of sand for this part of the imagined journey.

310
00:31:53,032 --> 00:32:00,576
Give it a place among the material moving downstream, then allow the river to open into coastal water.

311
00:32:00,732 --> 00:32:04,917
Here, waves and currents join the river’s work.

312
00:32:04,867 --> 00:32:10,338
Sand, gravel and finer particles can settle, then move again.

313
00:32:10,338 --> 00:32:14,882
The shapes available to living organisms change with them.

314
00:32:19,036 --> 00:32:22,883
The Elwha provides a documented example.

315
00:32:22,833 --> 00:32:29,583
Dam removal released trapped sediment and changed coastal habitats near the river mouth.

316
00:32:29,583 --> 00:32:37,377
United States Geological Survey studies found different biological responses in different places.

317
00:32:37,377 --> 00:32:42,510
Some changes created habitat for soft-bottom organisms.

318
00:32:42,510 --> 00:32:46,598
The effects were not uniform across the coast.

319
00:32:46,756 --> 00:32:50,588
Let your imagined grain settle for a moment.

320
00:32:50,538 --> 00:32:56,185
Its smallness belongs within a change large enough to reshape habitat.

321
00:32:56,185 --> 00:33:01,244
Then allow the view to widen beyond this particular river mouth.

322
00:33:05,396 --> 00:33:11,875
Olympic’s coast continues through beaches, rocky headlands and offshore stacks.

323
00:33:11,825 --> 00:33:15,737
Water works upon these larger forms too.

324
00:33:15,737 --> 00:33:24,517
Farther along the Pacific shore, an opening in the rock near Rialto offers a way to look through the land toward the sea.

325
00:33:24,668 --> 00:33:28,618
Picture an arch of rock framing the Pacific.

326
00:33:28,568 --> 00:33:36,142
At Hole-in-the-Wall, north of Rialto Beach, erosion has made an opening through which the sea can be seen.

327
00:33:36,308 --> 00:33:42,993
In this imagined view, let the dark edge of the rock give shape to the brighter water beyond.

328
00:33:42,943 --> 00:33:48,987
You might give the scene a wave sound, arriving and withdrawing at its own pace.

329
00:33:54,148 --> 00:33:59,392
Headlands, beaches and offshore stacks make a varied shoreline.

330
00:33:59,342 --> 00:34:04,710
Differences in rock and exposure influence how erosion shapes it.

331
00:34:04,710 --> 00:34:09,195
The resulting forms also offer places for life.

332
00:34:09,364 --> 00:34:17,505
Offshore islands provide nesting habitat for seabirds, including common murres and tufted puffins.

333
00:34:17,455 --> 00:34:24,617
Their feeding waters and protected islands belong to a marine system extending beyond the beach.

334
00:34:24,772 --> 00:34:31,060
Let your attention rest on an offshore shape, then return toward the nearer rock.

335
00:34:31,010 --> 00:34:38,025
Somewhere within this imagined shore, a small basin holds water left by a falling tide.

336
00:34:42,188 --> 00:34:46,814
The mountains and that basin differ enormously in size.

337
00:34:46,764 --> 00:34:54,044
Both invite the same close attention to how a place’s physical shape influences the lives within it.

338
00:34:54,212 --> 00:34:58,059
Imagine looking into a pool among the rocks.

339
00:34:58,009 --> 00:35:04,156
There is a small surface within the larger shore, holding its own reflections.

340
00:35:04,156 --> 00:35:10,186
Around it, water has withdrawn and exposed neighboring places to the air.

341
00:35:10,340 --> 00:35:13,025
Give the scene a little time.

342
00:35:12,975 --> 00:35:19,284
Notice the difference between what remains submerged and what the tide has left uncovered.

343
00:35:24,436 --> 00:35:31,989
Height, wave exposure and the shape of the shore influence the conditions organisms experience.

344
00:35:31,939 --> 00:35:38,542
Barnacles, mussels, seaweeds and other lives occupy this changing boundary.

345
00:35:38,542 --> 00:35:46,557
A small difference in position can bring a substantial difference in time underwater, temperature or exposure.

346
00:35:46,708 --> 00:35:51,261
The pool before you is one moment in a repeating cycle.

347
00:35:51,211 --> 00:35:55,946
A visit at another time would offer another part of its story.

348
00:36:00,116 --> 00:36:07,890
National Park Service monitoring returns to intertidal communities and measures conditions over time.

349
00:36:07,840 --> 00:36:14,634
Those repeated observations help distinguish an unusual day from a lasting change.

350
00:36:14,804 --> 00:36:18,475
Keep the little pool within the wider coast.

351
00:36:18,425 --> 00:36:25,454
The act of returning to look again connects it with the glacier, the meadow and the restored river.

352
00:36:25,454 --> 00:36:30,572
Across those places, evidence grows through patient attention.

353
00:36:30,740 --> 00:36:35,866
Let a few places return without trying to gather every detail.

354
00:36:35,816 --> 00:36:38,581
White ice above a valley.

355
00:36:38,581 --> 00:36:41,272
Meadow light near a burrow.

356
00:36:41,272 --> 00:36:44,125
Roots around fallen wood.

357
00:36:44,125 --> 00:36:47,096
A river moving toward the shore.

358
00:36:47,252 --> 00:36:53,776
Each scene is familiar now, and each continues beyond the moment we have imagined.

359
00:36:58,876 --> 00:37:03,414
Monitoring follows selected indicators through time.

360
00:37:03,364 --> 00:37:10,408
Focused research investigates questions and mechanisms those records alone may not explain.

361
00:37:10,408 --> 00:37:13,938
A baseline helps make change visible.

362
00:37:13,938 --> 00:37:18,320
Explaining why it happened can require further work.

363
00:37:18,484 --> 00:37:23,390
An observed trend and its cause are different parts of an account.

364
00:37:23,340 --> 00:37:28,369
A finding from one place or interval may not apply everywhere.

365
00:37:28,369 --> 00:37:34,737
And something this journey has not discussed is not necessarily unknown to science.

366
00:37:34,900 --> 00:37:42,791
The final six places in our spiral are open investigations built from the evidence already introduced.

367
00:37:42,741 --> 00:37:50,535
They are questions framed for this course, rather than an official ranking of the park’s research priorities.

368
00:37:53,692 --> 00:37:56,907
You can let them remain questions.

369
00:37:56,857 --> 00:38:02,577
We will return to a few places and notice where understanding can still grow.

370
00:38:02,577 --> 00:38:06,930
First, picture the glacier above the stream again.

371
00:38:07,084 --> 00:38:13,887
Bring back the pale surface of ice, then the narrow movement of water leaving the high country.

372
00:38:13,837 --> 00:38:21,013
Let the scene shift toward late summer, when the contribution of stored ice can matter downstream.

373
00:38:21,172 --> 00:38:25,460
Measurements document changes in glacier storage.

374
00:38:25,410 --> 00:38:32,940
Meltwater contributes to summer flow, alongside water from snow, rain and groundwater.

375
00:38:32,940 --> 00:38:37,425
Conditions within the channel influence the response too.

376
00:38:41,588 --> 00:38:52,303
Here is the question to carry through this scene: how will declining glacier storage change late-summer flow and temperature in individual Olympic watersheds?

377
00:38:52,412 --> 00:38:57,112
The uncertainty concerns local timing and magnitude.

378
00:38:57,062 --> 00:39:00,900
It does not undo the evidence of ice loss.

379
00:39:01,052 --> 00:39:10,428
Picture several records considered together: glacier measurements, streamflow, water temperature and weather from the same basin.

380
00:39:10,378 --> 00:39:16,746
Their relationships could help test explanations of the water arriving downstream.

381
00:39:16,746 --> 00:39:21,790
The resulting answer would need to include what remains uncertain.

382
00:39:25,956 --> 00:39:30,332
Leave the stream moving, with the question beside it.

383
00:39:30,282 --> 00:39:39,562
The conditions that shape its flow also lead attention toward the slopes where plants grow, and the changing season they have available.

384
00:39:39,732 --> 00:39:44,814
Return to the edge of the meadow, where open ground meets trees.

385
00:39:44,764 --> 00:39:50,529
Picture two nearby slopes: one sheltered, another more exposed.

386
00:39:50,529 --> 00:39:54,367
Give each enough space to keep its differences.

387
00:39:54,540 --> 00:40:02,505
Regional research has reported changes in plant timing and tree establishment in subalpine settings.

388
00:40:02,455 --> 00:40:11,175
Such findings help frame a question; they do not supply a complete explanation for every Olympic plant community.

389
00:40:15,340 --> 00:40:25,055
Where do changes in moisture, snow duration and disturbance alter these communities, and which processes explain the differences?

390
00:40:25,156 --> 00:40:28,988
Let a single seedling stand in the foreground.

391
00:40:28,938 --> 00:40:37,365
It is something to notice, but one seedling or one dry season cannot establish the direction of the whole community.

392
00:40:37,516 --> 00:40:46,481
An investigation could revisit vegetation plots across contrasting slopes and compare them with snow and weather records.

393
00:40:46,431 --> 00:40:52,254
More than one explanation would remain in view while the evidence was considered.

394
00:40:56,420 --> 00:41:01,767
Allow the meadow to remain a living place with an unfinished future.

395
00:41:01,717 --> 00:41:05,864
Among its plants is the burrow we pictured earlier.

396
00:41:05,864 --> 00:41:10,129
What would it mean to return and find no marmot in view?

397
00:41:10,292 --> 00:41:13,683
Picture the meadow on a quiet visit.

398
00:41:13,633 --> 00:41:18,898
The burrow is there, the plants moving lightly in your imagined wind.

399
00:41:18,898 --> 00:41:22,721
This time, no animal comes into view.

400
00:41:22,892 --> 00:41:29,357
Stay with the difference between that observation and the explanations it might suggest.

401
00:41:34,468 --> 00:41:39,094
A missed sighting is not proof that marmots have disappeared.

402
00:41:39,044 --> 00:41:47,677
Repeated visits help researchers distinguish imperfect detection from a change in which habitat patches are occupied.

403
00:41:47,677 --> 00:41:53,780
Occupancy concerns presence in a place; it is not a count of every animal.

404
00:41:53,932 --> 00:42:04,500
For a colony that is missing, how much might be explained by habitat conditions, predation, movement among patches or the limits of detection?

405
00:42:04,450 --> 00:42:08,553
Their contributions can differ among places.

406
00:42:08,716 --> 00:42:16,063
Imagine returning to the same meadow and comparing those visits with other carefully studied patches.

407
00:42:16,013 --> 00:42:23,292
Models of occupancy and permitted ecological studies could help evaluate the alternatives.

408
00:42:27,452 --> 00:42:31,211
One quiet hike leaves much unfinished.

409
00:42:31,161 --> 00:42:35,970
Careful attention can hold both concern and uncertainty.

410
00:42:35,970 --> 00:42:43,911
Carry that distinction toward the Elwha, where finding a fish also begins a larger question about its future.

411
00:42:44,084 --> 00:42:47,828
Follow the reopened river corridor again.

412
00:42:47,778 --> 00:42:52,852
Picture water continuing beyond a place where a dam once stood.

413
00:42:52,852 --> 00:42:58,822
Give that opening its full significance: access to habitat has changed.

414
00:42:58,988 --> 00:43:08,614
Surveys and environmental DNA have documented renewed use, with differences among fish species in timing and extent.

415
00:43:12,732 --> 00:43:17,579
Now allow the imagined view to extend through generations.

416
00:43:17,529 --> 00:43:20,264
A fish reaches a place.

417
00:43:20,264 --> 00:43:24,191
The next question is what that place supports.

418
00:43:24,348 --> 00:43:32,151
Which newly accessible habitats allow returning fish to reproduce and survive across generations?

419
00:43:32,101 --> 00:43:40,704
Passage, reproduction and long-term population trends are related, but each asks for its own evidence.

420
00:43:40,704 --> 00:43:46,704
Ocean conditions can influence returns as well as conditions within the river.

421
00:43:46,860 --> 00:43:55,736
An investigation could bring distribution surveys together with juvenile and adult monitoring and habitat measurements.

422
00:43:55,686 --> 00:44:01,686
That combination would help connect where fish are found with what happens over time.

423
00:44:05,836 --> 00:44:12,271
The successful passage remains important while the larger recovery question continues.

424
00:44:12,221 --> 00:44:15,707
Let the river reach the coast once more.

425
00:44:15,707 --> 00:44:21,692
There too, a visible change may have several influences working within it.

426
00:44:21,844 --> 00:44:25,868
Return to the small pool within the rocky shore.

427
00:44:25,818 --> 00:44:32,729
Picture it at another visit, then another: the same basin encountered through different seasons.

428
00:44:32,884 --> 00:44:37,863
The imagined sequence can help you hold a question about change.

429
00:44:37,813 --> 00:44:42,931
What would repeated observations reveal that one visit could miss?

430
00:44:47,084 --> 00:44:51,902
Monitoring records community composition and temperature.

431
00:44:51,852 --> 00:45:00,102
Coastal chemistry measurements help investigate ocean acidification and the conditions organisms experience.

432
00:45:00,102 --> 00:45:05,719
A change in abundance can coincide with several environmental shifts.

433
00:45:05,876 --> 00:45:15,414
How can researchers separate natural variability from warming, acidification and local disturbance at Olympic intertidal sites?

434
00:45:15,524 --> 00:45:20,415
The question does not make every explanation equally likely.

435
00:45:20,365 --> 00:45:26,498
It asks how evidence can distinguish their contributions in a particular setting.

436
00:45:26,498 --> 00:45:37,159
Biological surveys, temperature and chemistry records, comparisons among sites, and suitable tests of mechanisms could work together.

437
00:45:41,316 --> 00:45:46,663
A pattern can become visible before its causes are fully separated.

438
00:45:46,613 --> 00:45:49,716
Leave room for that patient work.

439
00:45:49,716 --> 00:45:58,848
Our final return moves inland to Lake Quinault, where useful questions must also connect with decisions about the lake’s care.

440
00:45:59,004 --> 00:46:03,189
Let Lake Quinault open between the trees once more.

441
00:46:03,139 --> 00:46:12,419
You might notice the surface first, then allow attention to include the habitats beneath it and the communities connected to its future.

442
00:46:12,588 --> 00:46:27,023
The Quinault Indian Nation’s planning and assessments identify ecological concerns, climate resilience and information gaps, including questions about blueback habitat and nearshore conditions.

443
00:46:31,132 --> 00:46:39,303
Which information would most improve decisions about habitat, water quality and resilience for blueback?

444
00:46:39,412 --> 00:46:47,244
A useful inquiry would identify a particular gap, the decision it affects and a suitable measurement.

445
00:46:47,194 --> 00:46:51,944
The Nation’s own assessments and priorities guide that learning.

446
00:46:51,944 --> 00:46:59,738
More data can inform a decision; it cannot automatically settle questions of values or governance.

447
00:46:59,908 --> 00:47:03,284
Let the lake keep its open question.

448
00:47:03,234 --> 00:47:06,896
There is no need to finish its story here.

449
00:47:12,060 --> 00:47:15,657
Now let the peninsula return around it.

450
00:47:15,607 --> 00:47:19,210
Lake Crescent holds a reflected mountain.

451
00:47:19,210 --> 00:47:22,710
Meadow light reaches the marmot’s burrow.

452
00:47:22,710 --> 00:47:28,666
In the Hoh and Queets, leaves and roots occupy different layers of the forest.

453
00:47:28,666 --> 00:47:33,975
Staircase and Sol Duc carry moving water through different valleys.

454
00:47:33,975 --> 00:47:40,210
Quinault and Elwha connect particular fish, places and people with the ocean.

455
00:47:40,380 --> 00:47:45,404
At the shore, a small pool waits within the rhythm of the tide.

456
00:47:51,516 --> 00:47:54,981
Choose one image that has stayed with you.

457
00:47:54,931 --> 00:48:01,695
It might be the wave passing over pale stones, or new roots reaching through a fallen tree.

458
00:48:01,695 --> 00:48:05,078
You can let the rest become background.

459
00:48:05,244 --> 00:48:09,370
Notice, if you wish, the support beneath you.

460
00:48:09,320 --> 00:48:13,394
The ordinary sounds of the place where you are listening.

461
00:48:13,394 --> 00:48:17,629
Allow your attention to return at an easy pace.

462
00:48:17,796 --> 00:48:28,628
The landscape can remain something you return to with curiosity: looking closely, following a connection, and leaving room for what is still being learned.
